# TLS server, CHUNKING reception with TLS shutdown mid-chunk
gnutls
#
# A TLS session that goes away while a BDAT chunk is still being read leaves the
# server holding readers, and a transfer buffer, belonging to a session that has
# ceased to exist.  Reading has to stop there.  Going on would either reach into
# the released buffer or read the rest of the connection in clear, on the socket
# the peer negotiated encryption for.
#
# Note for running this by hand: the daemon under test has to be the GnuTLS
# build.  Hand that binary to runtest and leave --tls off - that is the case in
# which the binary runtest was given is used at both ends, as $exim_client and as
# $exim_server alike.  Given --tls, runtest works in its cross-library mode
# instead: it picks the harness client program, and it also pairs the two
# library-specific builds against each other, so "--tls gnutls" leaves the
# OpenSSL build answering as $exim_server - and that build never frees the
# transfer buffer this test is about.  Test numbers spanning more than one script
# directory have to be asked for as a range, or one batch per directory.
#
exim -DSERVER=server -DSRV=tls -bd -oX PORT_D
****
#
# Chunk arithmetic - 85 declared, 84 sent, so one byte is still outstanding when
# the session goes.  The ">>> " prefix suppresses the trailing CRLF the client
# would otherwise add, so the payload is exactly the text after it:
#
#     To: Susan@random.com CRLF      22
#     From: Sam@random.com CRLF      22   (44)
#     CRLF, ending the header block   2   (46)
#     body-start-                    11   (57)
#     27 x A                         27   (84)
#
# Both halves of that shape are load-bearing.  The header block has to be
# complete, so that reception is past the header lines and into the body, which is
# where a released transfer buffer is read and written.  And the chunk has to be
# short, so that the session goes away with the transfer still owed data: a
# reader which treats what arrived as the whole of the message would accept it.
#
# The reply is matched in full rather than as just "421" because the receive
# timeout answers with a 421 of its own, and a loose match would be satisfied by
# the very behaviour this test exists to reject - a server that keeps reading the
# plain socket has nothing to read there, and says so when the timeout expires.
#
# Nothing is offered in clear after the shutdown, so the server has nothing
# unconsumed to reset over and its close is clean.  That is what lets the end of
# file be asserted here, and asserting it is half the point: a server which
# answered 421 and then left the socket usable would pass on the reply alone.
#
client-anytls 127.0.0.1 PORT_D
??? 220
EHLO rhu.barb
??? 250-
??? 250-SIZE
??? 250-8BITMIME
??? 250-PIPELINING
??? 250-CHUNKING
??? 250-STARTTLS
??? 250 HELP
STARTTLS
??? 220
EHLO rhu.barb
??? 250-
??? 250-SIZE
??? 250-8BITMIME
??? 250-PIPELINING
??? 250-CHUNKING
??? 250 HELP
MAIL FROM:<someone@some.domain>
??? 250
RCPT TO:<CALLER@test.ex>
??? 250
BDAT 85 LAST
>>> To: Susan@random.com\r\nFrom: Sam@random.com\r\n\r\nbody-start-AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA
stoptls
??? 421 Lost incoming connection
???*
****
millisleep 500
killdaemon
#
no_msglog_check
